<section id="research" class="data-section">
  <div class="container" style="position:relative">
    <div class="section-head reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">Data &amp; Research</p>
      <h2 style="margin:14px 0 20px">Why Surge exists.</h2>
      <p class="lead">Top-tier coaching has quietly become a luxury good. The numbers below explain the gap we're built to close. Full citations are listed in the bibliography.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-3">
      <!-- Card 1 -->
      <article class="card glass data-card reveal">
        <span class="badge">01 · Coaching Fees</span>
        <div class="big">$150–250</div>
        <h3>per hour for private technical coaching</h3>
        <p class="muted" style="margin-top:12px">Specialized 1-on-1 stroke and biomechanics coaches, particularly former elite competitors, commonly bill well above $120 an hour, with premium technical analysis reaching $150–$250. At two sessions a week, that is more than a car payment for one child's technique work. Meanwhile, families already spent an average of $1,016 on a child's primary sport in 2024, up 46% since 2019, roughly twice the rate of general inflation.</p>
        <p class="cite"><sup>1, 2</sup> Aspen Institute Project Play, <em>Parent Survey: Family Spending on Youth Sports</em> (2025); national swim-instruction pricing surveys (2025–26). See bibliography.</p>
      </article>

      <!-- Card 2 -->
      <article class="card glass data-card reveal">
        <span class="badge">02 · Household Pressure</span>
        <div class="big">3.5%</div>
        <h3>rise in consumer prices in the year to June 2026</h3>
        <p class="muted" style="margin-top:12px">Inflation has not returned to the 2% target. The Consumer Price Index rose 3.5% over the year ending June 2026, with shelter up 3.3% and food up 3.0%, on top of the 2021–2023 surge that permanently raised the baseline cost of housing, groceries, and utilities. When fixed costs climb faster than wages, discretionary line items like private sports lessons are the first cut in a middle-income budget.</p>
        <p class="cite"><sup>3, 4</sup> U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, <em>Consumer Price Index, June 2026</em> and <em>December 2025</em> releases. See bibliography.</p>
      </article>

      <!-- Card 3 -->
      <article class="card glass data-card reveal">
        <span class="badge">03 · The Access Gap</span>
        <div class="big">20.2 pts</div>
        <h3>participation gap between high- and low-income kids</h3>
        <p class="muted" style="margin-top:12px">In 2012, the gap in regular sports participation between children in households earning under $25,000 and those earning $100,000+ was 13.6 percentage points. By 2024 it had widened to 20.2 points. Children from the wealthiest homes are now twice as likely to play travel sports, and lower-income kids are far more likely to quit for cost reasons. Cost, not talent, is deciding who keeps improving.</p>
        <p class="cite"><sup>5, 6</sup> Aspen Institute Project Play, <em>State of Play 2025</em>, with Sports &amp; Fitness Industry Association (SFIA) participation data. See bibliography.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

